# Chapter 7 · Postmodern and Post-structural Feminism

> Postmodern feminism is the hardest school to grasp, and one of the most tested. It says there is **no single "woman"**, no single cause of oppression and no single truth; identities are **made in language and discourse**, and they **shift**. Two MCQs (2018, 2023) asked what post-structural feminism emphasises; the answer both times was **the contingent and discursive nature of identities**. A 2019 MCQ linked **gender equality constructed through literature** to **postmodern feminism**. The school was a short note in 2019.

## 7.1  Two hard words made simple

::: define Contingent | as used in the MCQ
Not fixed or necessary; **depending on circumstances**, and able to be otherwise. A contingent identity is one that could have been different and can change.
:::

::: define Discursive | as used in the MCQ
Produced by **discourse** — the ways a society **talks, writes and thinks** about something, including the rules about who may speak and what counts as true. A discursive identity is one **made by language and ideas**, not by nature.
-> So "the contingent and discursive nature of identities" means: **who we are is shaped by the language of our time and place, and can change**.
:::

## 7.2  Postmodernism in general

**Modernism** here means the Enlightenment belief in **reason, science, progress and universal truths**. Liberal feminism (all humans have reason and rights) and Marxist feminism (history moves towards the freedom of the oppressed class) are both "modern" theories.

**Postmodernism**, from the late 1960s onwards, doubts these big claims.

::: thinker Jean-François Lyotard | *The Postmodern Condition* (1979)
Lyotard defined the postmodern as "**incredulity toward metanarratives**" — disbelief in the grand stories, such as progress, reason or class revolution, that claim to explain everything.
-> For feminists, "patriarchy explains all women's oppression" is also a grand story.
:::

Two other French thinkers supplied the tools:

- **Jacques Derrida** (*Of Grammatology*, ==1967==) developed **deconstruction**: Western thought is built on pairs of opposites — reason/emotion, culture/nature, **man/woman** — in which the first term is ranked above the second. Deconstruction exposes and unsettles these ranked pairs. (Topic 1, Chapter 7 applied this to the word "gender".)
- **Michel Foucault** (*Discipline and Punish*, ==1975==; *The History of Sexuality*, ==1976==) argued that **power and knowledge** are joined. Power is not only held by kings or men; it runs through **discourses** — of medicine, psychiatry, law, education — that define what is "normal" and train our bodies and selves.

::: simple
A **structuralist** thinks meanings are fixed by a stable structure, like the rules of a language. A **post-structuralist** says meanings are **never fully fixed**: words get their meaning from their differences from other words, and those meanings shift with time and power. "Woman" meant something different in 1850 London, 1950 Lahore and 2026 Karachi.
:::

## 7.3  What postmodern feminism says

::: define Postmodern feminism | the school of difference and discourse
The view that there is **no universal category "woman"** and no single cause of women's oppression. Gender identities are **constructed in language and discourse**, are **multiple and unstable**, and differ by culture, class, race and history. The task is to **deconstruct** the binaries (man/woman, reason/emotion) and discourses that make women "the Other", and to value **plurality and difference**.
-> Key words: anti-essentialism, difference, discourse, deconstruction, plurality, local knowledge, performativity.
:::

Five core ideas:

1. **Anti-essentialism.** There is no "essence" of womanhood shared by all women.
2. **Difference.** Women differ by class, race, religion, sexuality, age, nation. Feminism must recognise these differences, not hide them.
3. **Language constructs reality.** Our ideas of man and woman are made in language; changing language can change reality.
4. **Suspicion of grand theories.** No single theory — liberal, Marxist or radical — explains all women's oppression.
5. **Local and personal knowledge.** Women's **particular experiences and stories** are valued over universal claims. This is why a 2025 MCQ on "the value of women's personal experiences" has postmodern feminism as its suggested key.

::: fig pyramid | Postmodern feminism's method: from the surface to what lies beneath.
Surface: "woman" seems natural and the same everywhere
Language: the words and binaries that rank man over woman
Discourse: the rules of medicine, law, religion and media that define "normal"
Power: whose interests these definitions serve
Plurality: many womanhoods, open to change
:::

## 7.4  The thinkers

::: thinker Judith Butler | *Gender Trouble* (1990); *Bodies That Matter* (1993)
Butler argued that gender is **performative**: there is no inner gender that our actions express; **repeated acts create** the appearance of a natural gender. She also criticised feminism's reliance on the category "women", which she said excludes those who do not fit its norms.
-> The most cited post-structural feminist (Topic 1, Chapter 8).
:::

- **Hélène Cixous, Luce Irigaray and Julia Kristeva** (Chapter 5) — the "French feminists" — are post-structuralist too. They deconstruct male-centred language and call for **écriture féminine**. Their work explains the MCQ link between **gender equality and literature**: writing is where new meanings of woman are made.
- **Jane Flax** ("Postmodernism and Gender Relations in Feminist Theory", ==1987==) argued that feminist theory is itself a kind of postmodern philosophy, because it questions the "neutral" male subject.
- **Joan W. Scott** ("Gender: A Useful Category of Historical Analysis", ==1986==; "Deconstructing Equality-versus-Difference", ==1988==) showed that the choice "equal or different?" is a false binary — women need both.
- **Donna Haraway** ("A Cyborg Manifesto", ==1985==) used the image of the **cyborg** — part human, part machine — to reject pure natural identities.
- **Chris Weedon** (*Feminist Practice and Poststructuralist Theory*, ==1987==) explained post-structuralism for feminist practice.
- **Nancy Fraser and Linda Nicholson** ("Social Criticism without Philosophy", ==1988==) proposed a **middle way**: keep large-scale social criticism, but make it historical and aware of difference.

::: fig timeline | Milestones of postmodern and post-structural feminism.
@layout vertical
1967: Derrida, Of Grammatology — deconstruction
1975: Cixous, "The Laugh of the Medusa"; Foucault, Discipline and Punish
1979: Lyotard — "incredulity toward metanarratives"
1985: Haraway, "A Cyborg Manifesto"
1986: Scott, "Gender: A Useful Category of Historical Analysis"
1987: Flax; Weedon
1988: Spivak, "Can the Subaltern Speak?"
1990: Butler, Gender Trouble; Nicholson (ed.), Feminism/Postmodernism
1995: Benhabib, Butler and others debate in Feminist Contentions
:::

## 7.5  Postmodern and modern feminisms compared

::: fig compare | Modern feminisms and postmodern feminism.
## Modern feminisms
- One category: "women"
- One main cause: law, patriarchy or capitalism
- Universal truths and rights
- A single path to liberation
## Postmodern feminism
- Many womanhoods: no single "woman"
- Many forms of power, many sites
- Truths are local and made in discourse
- Plural, local struggles
:::

## 7.6  Strengths

1. **Inclusion.** It opened space for women left out by earlier theories — women of colour, poor women, Muslim women, lesbians, transgender people.
2. **Attention to language and media.** It explains how images, words and textbooks shape gender.
3. **No false universals.** It warns Western feminists against speaking for all women — a lesson for postcolonial and Muslim feminism (Chapter 8).
4. **Flexibility.** If identities are not fixed, change is always possible.

## 7.7  Criticisms

1. **The "death of the subject".** **Seyla Benhabib** (in *Feminist Contentions*, ==1995==) warned that if the self is only a product of discourse, there is no one who can **act** and resist. Feminism needs agents.
2. **Political paralysis.** If there is no category "women", **on whose behalf** does feminism speak? Laws, quotas and statistics all need the category.
3. **Relativism.** If all truths are local, how can we condemn honour killing or child marriage as wrong everywhere?
4. **Jargon and elitism.** **Martha Nussbaum** ("The Professor of Parody", ==1999==) criticised Butler's obscure style and its distance from poor women's struggles.
5. **Neglect of material life.** Hunger, wages and violence are not only "discourse" (socialist criticism).

::: thinker Gayatri Chakravorty Spivak | Indian-born theorist · strategic essentialism
Spivak suggested a way out of the "political paralysis" problem: **strategic essentialism** — groups can **temporarily** act as if they share a single identity ("women", "workers") to win political goals, while knowing that the identity is not really fixed.
-> A 2026 MCQ asked what strategic essentialism suggests: **temporary strategic unity around an identity**.
:::

::: balance | Is postmodern feminism useful for a country like Pakistan?
**Useful:** it warns against speaking of "the Pakistani woman" as if a Karachi banker, a Tharparkar peasant, a Hazara widow and a *khawaja sira* shared one life. It exposes how discourses — of "honour", "modesty" or "Western agenda" — shape what women may do. **Limited:** its abstract language is far from urgent needs such as schools, clinics and protection from violence, and relativism can be used to excuse harmful customs. **The line to take:** use its insights on difference and discourse, and Spivak's strategic essentialism, while keeping universal human rights as the floor.
:::

## 7.8  Postmodern feminism and Pakistan: examples

- **Discourses of honour.** "*Ghairat*" is not a natural feeling but a discourse, taught and repeated, that defines a woman's body as her family's property. Deconstructing it is the first step to challenging honour crimes.
- **Media and advertising.** Fairness-cream adverts and TV dramas construct an ideal woman — fair, modest, self-sacrificing. Postmodern analysis shows how these images are made and whose interests they serve.
- **Literature.** Pakistani women poets such as **Kishwar Naheed** and **Fahmida Riaz** used poetry to challenge the language of female obedience. Kishwar Naheed's poem "We Sinful Women" (*Hum Gunahgar Auratein*) became an anthem of the 1980s women's movement; its English translation gave the title to an anthology edited by Rukhsana Ahmad (==1991==). This is feminism working through **literature** — the idea behind the 2019 MCQ.
- **Many womanhoods.** Class, province, language, sect and disability produce very different experiences of being a woman in Pakistan — the starting point of the intersectional analysis in Chapter 8.

::: eye | Short note on postmodern feminism (2019)
Define it (no single woman; identities contingent and discursive). Roots (Lyotard 1979, Derrida, Foucault). Thinkers (Butler 1990, Cixous 1975, Irigaray, Kristeva, Flax, Scott). Three or four core ideas. Two strengths. Two or three criticisms (Benhabib, Nussbaum). Spivak's strategic essentialism as the answer to the criticism. One Pakistani example (discourse of honour; women poets). A compare figure — modern versus postmodern — earns extra credit.
:::
